# Caddy in front of the app, terminating TLS.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHY A PROXY IS HERE AT ALL, RATHER THAN SERVING PLAIN HTTP
# ===========================================================================
#
# THE APP REFUSES PLAIN HTTP BY DESIGN, and it is right to. The admin session cookie is a
# bearer credential, the participant link token is one too, and the request bodies carry
# employees describing how their business runs. The rule is ONE check mounted before every
# route, with a single exemption for /healthz. A container that answered on plain HTTP
# would either be refused at every route or would have to weaken a rule that exists for a
# reason, so the honest shape is to terminate TLS in front - which is also what the real
# deployment does, behind the Tailscale proxy.
#
# `tls internal` USES CADDY'S OWN LOCAL CA. For a demo on one machine that is the right
# trade: no certificate to obtain, no domain to own, and the connection is genuinely
# encrypted. A browser will warn once that the certificate is not from a public authority,
# because it is not; accept it, or trust the CA with the command in README. Nothing here is
# suitable for a public hostname, and this file is not the deployment's configuration.
#
# THE PROXY SETS X-Forwarded-Proto: https, WHICH THE APP HONOURS ONLY BECAUSE TRUST_PROXY
# NAMES THIS CONTAINER. That is the other half of the trust boundary: the app resolves
# `req.secure` from the connection, and honours a forwarded protocol header only from a
# peer it was told to trust. Compose sets TRUST_PROXY to the Docker subnet this service
# sits on, so a client cannot forge the header and be believed.

{
	# The admin UI is run by an operator, not by browsers at scale, and a quieter log makes
	# a startup problem easier to see. Errors and warnings still go to stderr.
	admin off
	log {
		output stderr
		format console
		level INFO
	}
}

# THE SITE IS NAMED `localhost`, NOT LEFT AS A BARE `:443`.
#
# Caddy issues a certificate for the HOSTNAMES it serves, and a bare port with no name gives
# it nothing to put in the certificate - measured, the handshake then failed with
# "tlsv1 alert internal error" and not one log line said why, because there was no hostname
# to complain about. Naming `localhost` is also what makes the certificate match the URL the
# browser opens, which is what stops a mismatch warning on top of the untrusted-CA warning.
#
# `https://` IS THE PATH THE APP REQUIRES. See the top of this file.
https://localhost {
	tls internal

	# PROXIED TO THE APP BY SERVICE NAME ON THE COMPOSE NETWORK. The app is not published to
	# the host at all, so the only way in is through this proxy - which is what makes the
	# forwarded header trustworthy rather than merely present.
	reverse_proxy forgeright:3000 {
		# The app reads the client address for its rate limiter and its loopback check, so
		# the original address is passed through rather than replaced by the proxy's.
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
	}

	# A health check that reflects the app's own, so `docker compose ps` shows the whole
	# chain as healthy rather than only the container behind the proxy.
	handle /healthz {
		reverse_proxy forgeright:3000
	}
}
